# charon integration step 51 after 546078ea5d: commit e0a15d7a06a89c3609e19e565de7ac3b841c0c13
# first parent c3b42d30b55077287274c1ffee41db558c0aa37a
# series charon: patch -p1 in charon/ of the upstream source
#
# charon: trait references in signatures keep the lifetimes of the clauses they prove
#
# hax resolves the trait references of an item's clauses on predicates whose lifetimes are erased
# (solve_item_traits_inner). In bodies these lifetimes are unified afterwards; in signatures and
# type declarations they stay erased, both in the predicate a trait reference proves and in the
# arguments of the impl it names. Aeneas rejects the result ("Expected a runtime region type"):
# - lifted associated types are computed from those impls, so they lose the lifetime they borrow
#   from: `Metadata::values` (chelis_deep annotations.rs:669) returned
#   `FlatMap<Iter<'a, _>, Values<'a, _, _>, F, &'_ u64, Values<'_, _, _>>`;
# - closure types keep their clauses (remove_adt_clauses exempts them), and in a signature the
#   trait reference of the clause `V: FnMut(u32, Option<&'a str>)` of `closure<'a, V>` proved
#   `V: FnMut(u32, Option<&'_ str>)`.
#
# expand_associated_types now snapshots the trait clauses of every item and the header of every
# impl before the pass (PristineClauses). Before it computes the associated types of an item
# reference, it fills the erased lifetimes of each of its trait references from the clause the
# reference proves, instantiated with the arguments of the item reference: those of the proved
# predicate and, for an impl, those of the impl arguments (matching the impl header against the
# clause), then recursively those of the trait references proving the impl's clauses (depth at
# most 32). Only erased positions are filled; other lifetimes are kept.
#
# Test: ui/lift-assoc-types-signature-regions (new; `&'_ u64` and `Values<'_>` before, `'a` now).
# Other UI outputs (14 files): only trait references that had an erased lifetime change, which
# now carries the lifetime of the argument whose clause they prove (`{built_in impl Sized for &'_ T}`
# becomes `&'a T`, `{impl Clone for &'_0 T}<'_, U>` becomes `<'_0, U>`), in signatures, in the
# names of impls whose header contains one, and in one body type of associated-types.

